OL,2,Vj5{5jj5~sGAMBULANCE STAFF VOTE
OL,3,Vj5op0ozz5pGTO ACCEPT PAY DEAL
OL,4,D
OL,5, Ambulance staff have voted in a
OL,6, national ballot by 81% in favour of
OL,7, accepting a two-year pay settlement.
OL,9,FAmbulance officers and control room
OL,10,Fassistants voted by 92% for the deal.
OL,12,FThe offer is said by the unions to be
OL,13,Fworth 17.6% but the Government has
OL,14,Fput the figure nearer 13%.
OL,16,FChief union negotiator Roger Poole said
OL,17,Fthere would be an orderly return to
OL,18,Fwork by the 22,500 staff on Friday
OL,19,Fmorning.
OL,22,D]F This Ceefax page is fromG13-03-90
OL,23,D]F Much more at: teletextarchive.com
OL,24,ANext PageBT'text FilesCRewindFReg Radio
FL,182,190,120,250,0,100
PN,18111
SC,0011
CT,8,C
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F181A1773426106
OL,1,Vk4          j7   h4             G11/15
OL,2,Vj5{5jj5~s j7*u?j?h7`s}
OL,3,Vj5op0ozz5p juj5j5j5ju
OL,5, Chief ambulance negotiator Roger Poole
OL,6, said the offer accepted by ambulance
OL,7, workers vindicated the ambulance
OL,8, workers' campaign.
OL,10,FIt was considerably better than the
OL,11,FGovernment's original 6.5% offer,
OL,12,Fhe said.
OL,14,FMr Poole said union members had
OL,15,Foverwhelmingly accepted the belief
OL,16,Fof their negotiators that it was not
OL,17,Fpossible to win a pay formula.
OL,19,FBut he insisted that the unions would
OL,20,Fnot drop their demands for a pay
OL,21,Fformula as the best way forward.
OL,23,T]FFromG13-03-90F/ teletextarchive.com
